With „Anthology“, Dutch photographer Yaël Temminck looks back on more than a decade of portraits that trace the people defining music, fashion and contemporary art on their own terms. Across 124 black-and-white photographs, familiar names like Kim Gordon, Nan Goldin, Yohji Yamamoto and John Cale appear alongside a younger generation including julie, Wisp and Xaviersobased. Rather than separating established figures from emerging ones, Temminck places them in the same conversation, making the book feel as much about cultural exchange as portraiture itself.
What stands out is the restraint. The photographs don’t rely on spectacle or carefully constructed personas. They’re intimate without feeling intrusive and leave space for the people in front of the camera to exist as themselves. In a moment when so much imagery is produced for immediate attention, Anthology feels refreshingly patient.
Entirely self-funded and released as a limited edition of 200 hand-numbered copies, the book reflects the same independent approach that runs through Temminck’s work. Published by Veins Books and designed in collaboration with Ten Years Later Studio, it feels less like a traditional monograph and more like a document of a creative community that continues to evolve.
Anthology doesn’t try to define a generation. Instead, it quietly captures the people shaping it, reminding us that the most lasting portraits are often the ones that never ask for attention in the first place.
*Please note that some images have been cropped slightly to fit Instagram’s carousel format.
